// Here's how it works.
// As you probably know, this code is only executed when the player collides with a block.
// We use the collision with a block to set the "jump" variable back to false, which in turn
// tells the game to stop showing the jumping animations. However, there's a chance that
// the player will collide with an overhead block - a platform above him, for instance. If that
// happens, we don't want the game to stop displaying the jumping sprites.
// So, we have the code in two sections. The first line checks the y position of the block
// that the player has collided with. If the block's y position is greater than the player's
// y position - in other words, if the block is below the player - we set jump to false, telling
// the computer not to display the jumping sprites any more.
// If the player has collided with a block which isn't below him, he don't bother setting jump
// to false, meaning that he'll continue to display the jumping sprites.
// For more details on the move_contact command, check you GM manual, as it explains it better
// than I can.

